<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guhila dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Guhilots, Guhilot dynasty, Guhilas of Mewar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Guhilas were a Rajput dynasty that ruled Mewar in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> from the seventh century, first from Nagda and Ahar as feudatories of larger powers and later from Chittor. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> took Chittor in 1303 the main line ended, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodia</a> branch under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammir-singh">Hammir Singh</a> restored the dynasty around 1326; its descendants ruled Mewar, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a> State, until 1949.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Bardic legends have obscured the dynasty's beginnings. In the 7th century three Guhila houses ruled in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, at Nagda-Ahar, Kishkindha and Dhavagarta, and none claimed a grand origin. By the 10th century only the Nagda-Ahar line survived, and it then named its founder as Guhadatta, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> from Anandapura; the Atpur inscription of 977 lists 20 kings in unbroken succession from him to Shaktikumara. From the 13th century its records named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bappa-rawal">Bappa Rawal</a> as founder instead, whom historians identify variously with Kalabhoja, Shiladitya or Khummana. Scholars such as D. R. Bhandarkar argued that the family was originally Brahmin, a reading G. H. Ojha rejected.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The early kings ruled as feudatories of the Gurjara-Pratiharas. Shiladitya, attested by an inscription of 646, raised the family's standing, and R. V. Somani suggests that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and zinc mines at Jawar were opened in his reign. Bharttripatta II became independent and took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharajadhiraja">Maharajadhiraja</a>, attested in 943. Late in the 10th century the Paramara king Munja plundered Ahar, by then a second capital, and took eastern Mewar with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittor-fort">Chittorgarh</a>, and the Chahamana king Vakpati II later killed Ambaprasada.</p><p>In the 12th century the family split into a senior Rawal branch at Chittor and a junior Rana branch at Sisoda. Samantasimha was driven out by the Chahamana king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirtipala">Kirtipala</a>, probably during 1171–1179, and his brother Kumarasimha regained the kingdom by 1182. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaitrasimha">Jaitrasimha</a> (r. c. 1213–1252) the Guhilas reached a high standing, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iltutmish">Iltutmish</a> invaded Mewar and destroyed Nagda. Samarasimha helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghel">Vaghela</a> king Sarangadeva repel a Turkic invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> before 1285, and later bought off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a>'s army with tribute.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Rawal line ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratnasimha">Ratnasimha</a>'s defeat at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittor-fort">Chittorgarh</a> in 1303. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumbhalgarh">Kumbhalgarh</a> inscription of 1460, Lakshmasimha of the Rana branch died defending the fort after Ratnasimha left the field. The Rana branch, founded by Rahapa, a son of Ranasimha, later restored the family's rule over Mewar under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammir-singh">Hammir Singh</a>, and as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia-dynasty">Sisodia dynasty</a> it ruled Mewar until the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guhila dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mewar, known in its last centuries as Udaipur State, was the kingdom of the <b>Guhila dynasty</b> in the hills and valleys of southern Rajasthan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Maharawals of Dungarpur claimed descent from the elder line of the <b>Guhila dynasty</b> of Mewar, said to have moved south into the Vagad hills in the late 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/dungarpur-state">Dungarpur State</a></li></ul></section>
